<table border="0" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" bgcolor="#FFFFFF" width="100%"><tr><td>
<a href="modules.php?name=News&new_topic=20"><img src="images/topics/live.gif" border="0" Alt="Live" align="right" hspace="10" vspace="10"></a>
<div align=justify><font class="posted" color="#000000"><P><B>Madonna</B> gushed for the very first time about <B>Michael Moore</B>'s "Fahrenheit 9/11," Wednesday night. 
<P>
<P>"I have never cried so much in a movie in my life," she told her premiere audience in Madison Square Garden. "I urge each and every one of you to see it." 
<P>
<P>Addressing Moore, who was in the crowd, she said, "The world needs more people like you." 
<P>
<P>Madonna, who's slowly taking on the biblical name Esther among pals, saw the anti-<B>Bush</B> documentary Tuesday after bringing her daughter, <B>Lourdes</B>, to visit <B>Gwyneth Paltrow</B>'s baby, <B>Apple</B>. Paltrow was also in the audience, dancing the entire night. Madonna/Esther's hubbie, <B>Guy Ritchie</B>, and Kabbala kahuna <B>Yehuda Berg</B> grooved like teens to "Vogue," "Material Girl" and "Holiday." 
<P>
<P>Meanwhile, <B>Heidi Klum</B> kept track of messages (from boyfriend <B>Seal</B>?) on her Blackberry. And <B>Leonardo DiCaprio</B> was seen justifying his love for girlfriend <B>Gisele Bundchen</B>. Spywitnesses report that, except for a moment where Leo sang along to "Like A Prayer," the sinewy twosome couldn't keep their hands off each other. 
<P>
<P>Still, the world's top supermodel stopped long enough to take note of the 45-year-old legend's physique. "<STRONG>Look at [Madonna's] body," Bundchen was heard to say. "She's so toned!"</STRONG></P>
<P><STRONG>NYDailyNews</STRONG></P>
</font></div>
</td></tr></table>


</td></tr></table>


