<table border="0" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="0" bgcolor="#FFA8D3" width="100%"><tr><td>
<table border="0" cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" bgcolor="#FFA8D3" width="100%"><tr><td align="left">
<font class="storytitle" color="#FFFFFF"><b>SLYGUY' REVIEW</b></font>
</td></tr></table></td></tr></table>

<table border="0" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" bgcolor="#FFFFFF" width="100%"><tr><td>
<a href="modules.php?name=News&new_topic=20"><img src="images/topics/live.gif" border="0" Alt="Live" align="right" hspace="10" vspace="10"></a>
<div align=justify><font class="posted" color="#000000"><P><STRONG>Our dear friend SlyGuy former moderator of the glorious Madonna's&nbsp; dotmusic forum, attended yeaterday the NY first concert at Madison Square Garden</STRONG>.</P>
<P>Here is his review:</P>
<P><STRONG>Sorry, I'm a bit nutty and put a lot of extra comments in parenthesis. And I'm VERY long-winded and insanely detailed about things (even if they're insignificant details you don't care about it), so if you haven’t seen the show and are trying to avoid in-depth details, I’d advise you not read my review! I'm putting this in my scrapbook so I'll forever remember it.<BR></STRONG><BR>----<BR><BR>I've seen a lot of concerts throughout my life at <STRONG>Madison Square Garden (including the D.W. Tour, which I enjoyed</STRONG>), but never in my life have I seen <STRONG>such a f**king amazing show</STRONG> on so many levels (singing, dancing, visuals, sheer spectacle) there in which the entire crowd (except for some soccer school moms' stick-in-the mud husbands) was excitedly standing up from beginning to end and giving back the performer so much energy. 20,000 people (I looked and walked around the arena a lot before it started, that arena was very packed and suffocating up to the $45 seat nosebleeds, and especially on the floor, <STRONG>so don't listen to the NY Post/FAUX News nonsense about the NY shows selling very poorly <!--emo&:manson:--><IMG style="VERTICAL-ALIGN: middle" alt=manson.gif src="http://67.18.37.16/431/162/emo/manson.gif" border=0><!--endemo--> ) singing loudly along to "Like A Prayer" and running off into the space of the aisles to dance hysterically during "Into The Groove". </STRONG>It's so surreal to look back on now. I'm trying to remember the show now, but the experience was so incredibly draining and exhilarating and I'm getting overwhelmed with so many fantastic flashbacks.<BR><BR>From the moment the lights dim (<STRONG>the show started precisely at 8:41 P.M. and ended at about 10:40pm</STRONG>) and the multiple mobile screens come to life, the crowd starts flipping out for their Queen of course. The screens move around and come together like a puzzle. They show a lot of fabulously psychotically edited footage (most of which is new and a lot not even shown in the exhibit) from the <STRONG>Steven Klein photo shoot</STRONG>. Incredibly creepy music seeps through the arena and the screen images get weirder. The stage and set is truly amazing. It looks like an exhibit from <STRONG>The Metropolitan Museum of Art</STRONG>. There is so much going on and it's gorgeous. "An assault on the senses" indeed.<BR><BR>"<STRONG>The Beast Within" version of "Justify My Love" starts.</STRONG> We don't immediately see Madonna on stage, but we know her presence is very near, and we New Yawkers are screaming hysterically for their girl to come the f**k out already. <I>"Behold, I am coming soon..."</I> God that line really made me shiver and melt at the same time. I might be mistaken here (because so much was going on and I was floored, so my memory might be fuzzy), but <STRONG>then Madonna is on a platform </STRONG>that slowly rises about 10-feet above the stage. Even though I've seen her live and up-close before, it's still so incredibly surreal (and VERY exciting! ) to experience her three-dimensionally. <STRONG>She does all of these insane headstands, body pretzels and scorpions outdoing the moves from the contortionists on the Drowned World Tour</STRONG>. As stunning as is this scene, she's just warming up her temple of a body for the shenanigans later in the show.<BR><BR>She launches into “Vogue” fabulously flipping her head and lovely R.O.L. hair back. What a great opening image that is of her. Christ, she looked so hot there. The crowd is on their feet dancing and singing along cheerfully, especially during the name-dropping rap. Even though she’s older these days, her vogue-ing is still f**king fierce and executed with great energy. I<STRONG> think this was better than the Girlie Show and Blond Ambition Tour performances. Besides the show-woman herself, there is so much other stuff going on the stage with the multiple screen projections, dancers and sets. I love that the dancers do that patronizing little clap for Madonna like the dancers did at the 1990 MTV Awards performance of “Vogue”.<BR></STRONG><BR>All the dancers depart the stage and Madonna performs a super-thumping-bass <STRONG>“Nobody Knows Me.</STRONG>” Of all the songs tonight, I was not expecting this to be so damn cool! That moving conveyor belt on the stage was even better than the screen projections! She looked so f**king fierce working it all by herself (no dancers were on stage), and singing this live with the vocoder, all together worked really well, much better than I anticipated. I kept screaming, “Work it bitch!” during this performance. I loved how she jumped and moved around in random (I’m sure it’s choreographed but she still makes it look freestyle, that’s how damn good she is), sudden changes of direction on the conveyor belt. She was so cheerful and excited! <STRONG>On the screen projections there was Matrix-like images flashing about, but I was too busy watching her work it. I was marveling at how power over the crowd – seeing her work it like that all by herself with no dancers on stage.<BR></STRONG><BR>She melts my heart for <STRONG>“Frozen</STRONG>”. The screen projections are showing two naked people embracing and getting intimate. I think I read some people complain that her voice was wobbly and haggard at some other show on the tour, but her voice was so strong and clear tonight! Maybe as the tour went along her voice adapted to sing it more efficiently, I don’t know, I’m not an expert on these things, but it sounded great. <STRONG>Even better than the Drowned World Tour rendition of this song (which I do like too). She was emoting it a bit, but that’s a plus for me because I love that style.<BR><BR>The military section</STRONG>. The dancers (who are very hot by the way) are dressed as army men and doing push-ups on stage while the sounds of helicopters, guns and bombs are blasting through the arena. I think Madonna looked her best and most f**kable in this section.<STRONG> Goddamn she looked so hot in those fatigues, beret and with her hair, mmm mmm good.</STRONG> Um, oh yeah, she sings “American Life”. I like this mix much better than the derided album version. There is a lot of war imagery on the screen projections. There’s so much going on before your eyes and into your ears that it’s hard to keep up. At one point she seems like she’s having a nervous breakdown throwing herself to the ground and she really lets loose with her voice. The crowd was applauding the anti-war statements.<BR><BR>During the military section the catwalk descends from the ceiling! My friend was hysterical when she saw come it down and hook up to the stage. More dancers come out dressed in fabulous trampily re-invented burkas and nun outfits. Madonna and all (or most?) of the dancers march up and cavort in the V-catwalk. It’s so cool! If you have seats halfway down the floor and further back in the arena you will enjoy this a lot because the catwalk is quite long and you’ll see Madonna come close towards you. Donna looked so cute singing on stage in her military fatigues and with her sassy short hair. Siedah looked cool herself!<BR><BR><I>“<STRONG>Come on boys, do you believe in love?!”</STRONG></I><STRONG> Good God yes, especially when you’re singing about it Madge! This is one of my favorite performances of the night! The choreography they do with the rifles is awesome! Engraved in my memory is her singing <I>“What you need is a big strong hand … To lift you to your higher ground … Make you feel like a queen on a throne … Make him love you till you can't come down …”</I> while she’s hoisted up into the air by her male dancers while sitting on a pair of rifles they’re carrying.</STRONG></P>
<P><STRONG><BR>&nbsp;Read the second part of the most realible and exciting review <A href="http://www.madonnavillage.it/modules.php?name=Forums&amp;file=viewtopic&amp;p=100717#100717">here</A></STRONG></P>
</font></div>
</td></tr></table>


</td></tr></table>


